-  ['What does property cost?','It depends heavily on the area. Premium coastal spots like Kololi and Cape Point run roughly $1,000–$1,200 per m², mid-range areas such as Bijilo and Kotu $850–$900 per m², and emerging areas like Brufut, Sanyang and Tanji $350–$650 per m². Bare land starts far lower, from around $15–25 per m² in developing areas.'],
+  ['Can foreigners buy property in The Gambia?','A non-Gambian can hold Gambian property, but not without restriction. New freehold is not available to anyone; what foreign buyers actually take is an assignment of an existing state lease (typically 99 years), and every such assignment needs the Minister’s written consent. Upcountry the Lands (Regions) Act caps a non-indigene at 50 years. Have a Gambian lawyer confirm the position in writing before you commit — the full, sourced answer is in our FAQ and the guide on buying as a foreigner.'],
+  ['What does property cost?','It depends heavily on the area, and the honest answer is a table rather than a sentence: our price page shows the measured asking price per m² of land for every area we cover, in dalasi with a currency switcher, and says how many listings each figure rests on. Coastal Kombo is the dearest; prices fall quickly a few kilometres inland and again upcountry.'],
   ['How do I know the title is genuine?','This is the single biggest risk in Gambian property, so we treat it as step one. On request we commission a document review: a qualified specialist reads the title deed and ownership documents and confirms the document type before you commit. We still recommend your own solicitor and a licensed surveyor for the boundaries.'],
-  ['What’s the difference between freehold, leasehold and customary title?','Freehold gives full, permanent ownership and is the strongest form of title. Leasehold — often 99 years — is common on state land and is renewable. Customary or “Alkalalo” land is allocated by a village head under traditional law; it can sometimes be converted to a registered lease. Our verification confirms which one applies before you buy.'],
+  ['What’s the difference between freehold, leasehold and customary title?','Freehold is a closed, pre-1945 class: new freeholds cannot be created and a document offered as a new coastal freehold is a warning sign. State leasehold — usually 99 years — is the normal title on the coast; renewal is at the Minister’s discretion, not a right. Customary or “Alkalo” land is allocated by a village head under traditional law and can sometimes be converted to a registered lease. An Ownership Verification confirms which one applies before you buy.'],
   ['Do I need a land survey before I buy?','Yes — for every plot. A licensed surveyor confirms the exact boundaries and area and checks for encroachment or overlapping claims, the most common source of land disputes in The Gambia. Your solicitor can arrange this alongside the title check.'],
-  ['Can I get a mortgage or financing for land?','Mortgage financing for bare land is limited in The Gambia — most buyers pay cash or agree instalments directly with the seller. Ask your MyKunda advisor about current bank financing options if you need them.'],
+  ['Can I get a mortgage or financing for land?','Mortgage financing for bare land is limited in The Gambia — most buyers pay cash or agree instalments directly with the seller. No Gambian bank publishes its mortgage terms, so ask the bank directly — our mortgage guide explains what to ask.'],
   ['What do I need before I can start building?','Once you own the plot, you will need approved building plans and a permit from the local council before construction starts. Your surveyor’s report and title documents are usually required as part of that application.'],
   ['What extra costs should I budget for?','Plan for stamp duty and registration at the Lands Office, legal fees for the sale agreement and transfer, and a survey fee if boundaries need confirming. Your solicitor will quote these up front — there is no buyer commission on MyKunda.'],
-  ['Can I buy from abroad?','Yes, and many of our buyers do. We record video walkthroughs and share them over WhatsApp, coordinate with your solicitor, and can act as your eyes on the ground for surveys and handover.'],
+  ['Can I buy from abroad?','Yes. Everything on MyKunda works remotely: you message the seller, ask for a video walkthrough, and agree the price from wherever you are. For an independent look at the plot before you pay, order a Full Ownership Check — it includes a site visit by an independent specialist, with photographs and a GPS pin. MyKunda itself has no office and never handles your money.'],
   ['Is land a better buy than a finished house?','It can be, if you have time and someone reliable to manage the build. Plots in Brufut and Sanyang have appreciated strongly, but construction takes longer and costs more than most first-time buyers expect. A finished home is the simpler route.'],
 ];
